          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does asthma guidance describe as the stepwise approach, and what changed about reliever choice?",
          "answer": "Asthma treatment follows a stepwise approach, meaning treatment is adjusted based on a person's current symptom control and history of exacerbations, rather than being fixed. This approach is regularly reviewed (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nA significant change in modern guidance is that treating asthma with only a short-acting bronchodilator (SABA) is no longer considered appropriate at any severity. All adults and adolescents with asthma should receive therapy containing inhaled corticosteroids. This is because SABA-only treatment does not address the underlying inflammation and is linked to severe asthma attacks, even in people with infrequent symptoms. Frequent use of relievers (several canisters per year) is itself a sign of increased risk (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nRegarding reliever choice, there is a difference in guidance for mild asthma. Global strategy guidance suggests an inhaled corticosteroid-and-formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across all steps of asthma severity. However, some national panel guidance still considers a short-acting bronchodilator reliever, used alongside a regular controller medication, to be appropriate for mild disease (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
